{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model underpredicts the level by ~+1 to +3 EUR most of the time (especially days ~3–20). Apply an additive uplift that is larger in the mid-horizon than at the very front.",
    "Trend/impulse lag: after an upswing starts, the model follows with a delay and insufficient amplitude (misses the run-up into ~69–70). Use a momentum kicker: if last 5–10 days are rising, add extra uplift for the next ~10–15 forecast days.",
    "Late-horizon crash overshoot: forecasts often drop too far in the back end (days ~25–30), predicting a sharp selloff that does not materialize (true stays higher). Damp negative moves beyond ~3 weeks: shrink forecasted declines vs the last observed price by ~30–60%.",
    "Over-mean-reversion in the tail: the model reverts toward a lower mean too aggressively at longer horizons. Blend tail forecasts toward a higher anchor (e.g., recent 10–20d average) rather than extrapolating a downtrend.",
    "Asymmetry: model handles downside worse than upside—when it predicts a large drawdown, it is usually too large; when it predicts upside, it is too small. Apply asymmetric correction: amplify positive deviations modestly, but cap/attenuate negative deviations strongly, especially after recent strength."
  ]
}
